  "strategy": "1. Approach and Rationale\nI propose to solve the Erd\u0151s minimum overlap problem using a sparse grid optimization with iterative quadratic programming approach. Unlike gradient-based methods, this approach focuses on:\n\n- Using a multi-resolution representation that prioritizes regions with high auto-correlation\n- Combining continuous and discrete optimization to maintain feasibility\n- Employing a progressive evaluation and repair system to ensure global optimality\n- Exploiting dimension reduction to handle the non-convex optimization landscape\n\nThe key bottleneck addressed is the limited exploration caused by gradient-based methods, which can get stuck in local optima. This approach directly targets high-risk regions using adaptive grid embedding and ensures global feasibility through rigorous integral and boundary constraints.\n\n2. Mathematical Specification\n- Representation: n-point samples on [0,2] domain\n- Formulation: Minimize C\u2085 = max\u2096 \u222bh(x)(1-h(x+k))dx subject to:\n  1. \u222bh(x)dx = 1\n  2. 0 \u2264 h(x) \u2264 1\n- Objective: Estimated via discrete samples as: \n  C\u2085 = (2/n) * max\u2096 np.correlate(h, 1-h, mode='full')[k]\n- Attack Strategy: Embedded sparse grid with adaptive sampling weights. Solves convexified optimization at selected grid points.\n\n3. Implementation Procedure\n- Initialize with provided parent if available\n- Sparse grid initialization:\n  1. Compute decorrelation map: DM[k] = E[product of neighbors]\n  2. Create risk-sorted samples by sorting indices by DM\n  3. Embed initial samples in high-risk octaves first\n- Optimization cycle:\n  1. Active set selection via quadratic relaxation\n  2. Successive dimensionality reduction\n  3. Regularized optimize in reduced space\n  4. Boundary projection repair\n- Progressive refinement: Cycle through dimensionality reduction with updates to risk map\n- Termination: When maximum grid level reached or time exhausted\n\n4. Search Choices and Variations\n- Algo Mode Variations:\n  Phase Phases:\n    Primary: Active set reduction (alpha)\n    Secondary: Dimension hopping (beta)\n    Advanced: Fast propagation (gamma)\n  Selection rules:\n    - Grid density: 0.10 * (1 - (1 - alpha)^0.7)\n    - Risk tolerance: 0.005 * (distance to feasible)\n    - Neighborhood intensity: 0.05 * (gradient direction)\n\n5. Compute Budget and Robustness\n- Estimated wall time:\n  Primary grid embed: 4s\n  Progressive refine: 0.3s per grid level\n  Convex repair: 2s per grid level\n- Total estimates for n=700: ~35s\n- Deadline check integrated in grid cycle\n- Boundary fallback: Truncated projection (span of 3 points)\n\n6. Validation and Return Contract\n- Post-hoc checks:\n  - Integral exactness: normalize to sum(n/2)\n  - Boundary warp checking: clip(h[i] to [0,1])\n  - Phase stability: ensure no gradient explosion\n- Re-evaluation: Final solution sent through np.correlate() with mode=\"full\"\n- Prepare output as (improved_h, c5_min, n), clip all values to [0,1] and ensure sum= n/2",
